", "thinking_blocks": [], "responses_reasoning_item": null, "action": null, "tool_name": "end_researcher_turn", "tool_call_id": "chatcmpl-tool-a3356184bb4e75bf", "tool_call": {"id": "chatcmpl-tool-a3356184bb4e75bf", "responses_item_id": null, "name": "end_researcher_turn", "arguments": "{"answer": "FULL EXPERIMENT DESIGN (design-only; nothing executed; all numbers below are protocol constants, documentation facts, or planning estimates — none are results)\n\nD0. QUESTION AND DECISION RULE\nQuestion: does BAdam yield better zero-shot math performance than LoRA when finetuning Llama-3-70B on MathInstruct?\nPrimary endpoint (researcher decision 2): P = mean of zero-shot accuracy on GSM8K-test (1,319 items) and MATH-test (5,000 items). Per-benchmark accuracies always reported alongside P. If the two benchmarks disagree in direction, that disagreement is reported as a finding; no forced headline verdict. A claim of \"BAdam better\" requires the BAdam−LoRA difference on P to have a bootstrap 95% CI excluding zero; otherwise the verdict is \"no detectable difference.\"\nHypotheses: H1 = BAdam > LoRA on P; H0 = no detectable difference. Directionally, prior 7B-scale reporting motivates H1, but nothing at 70B is established — that is the gap this experiment addresses.\n\nD1. ARMS\nPrimary (practitioners' comparison — what the question literally asks):\n- A1 BAdam: Llama-3-70B base, full-parameter finetuning with BAdam (bounded-amplitude delta B, levels L, freezing period p as named hyperparameters; D4).\n- A2 LoRA: Llama-3-70B base, LoRA r=64, alpha=64, dropout 0.05, applied to all linear projections (q,k,v,o,gate,up,down); optimizer AdamW. Approximate trainable share (~0.1–0.3% of weights) computed from architecture at execution time, not quoted from memory.\n- A3 Unfinetuned control: base model, evaluation-only, identical prompts/decoding. No training cost; gives the \"did finetuning help at all\" baseline.\nSensitivity pair (researcher decision 1, flagged, single sensitivity analysis — a pair because neither method alone answers a comparison): S1 = {BAdam, LoRA} initialized from Llama-3-70B-Instruct, reusing hyperparameters chosen in D3 without re-searching, evaluated on primary endpoints only. The design states explicitly which initialization it assumes and why, so a reader whose intent was Instruct finds the relevant arm rather than a silent assumption.\nSecondary budget-matched arms (interpretive layer, feasibility-cuttable — decision 3):\n- B1 Scaled LoRA: r=256, all linear modules — raises LoRA's updated-parameter count toward BAdam's.\n- B2 Reduced-cycle BAdam: stop at the first incomplete freezing cycle so only a partial parameter fraction has ever received updates.\nFeasibility rule (decision 3): if only one budget arm is affordable, drop both extra arms and keep the accuracy-versus-update-budget curves, which require no additional training because the budget axes are measured from all completed runs (D5).\n\nD2. DATA AND CONTAMINATION (researcher decision 4 — strict)\nCorpus: MathInstruct (~394K instruction–solution pairs) as released, with three fixed exclusions.\n(a) Held-out selection slice: 2,000 MathInstruct samples (fixed seed) removed from training, used ONLY for hyperparameter/LR selection via assistant-token NLL. Benchmark test sets are never used for training or selection — stated as a hard invariant.\n(b) Near-duplicate screen vs GSM8K-test and MATH-test: normalized exact match plus token-shingle Jaccard and MinHash-LSH above pre-registered thresholds, including numeric-abstraction comparison (digit-masked text) because math near-duplicates often differ only in numbers. Flagged items are excluded from training.\n(c) Stratified reporting: every headline result is additionally reported split by whether each test item had a raw-corpus near-duplicate (exposed vs clean strata). Sensitivity run (feasibility-cuttable): one uncleaned-corpus training run of the cheaper LoRA arm; because the quantity of interest is the BAdam−LoRA difference, a single run estimates whether leakage shifts the gap, and it is the cheaper arm so the leak-audit costs less.\nPreprocessing: chat-style templating, loss on assistant tokens only, max length 4,096 with truncation rate reported; no curriculum filtering (assumption); 1 epoch per training run.\n\nD3. OPTIMIZER SELECTION — PRE-REGISTERED, SHARED BUDGET (researcher decision 5)\nThe BAdam paper did not establish B, L, p, or usable LR at 70B; these are named hyperparameters, not quoted values.\n- Search budget: ≤4 configs per method for LR on A1/A2; for BAdam, B and p each varied over 2 values at the median grid LR (≤2 additional configs). Each search run: 1 pass over 20% of the cleaned corpus; selection = lowest assistant-token NLL on the D2(a) slice. Chosen values are recorded as protocol outputs.\n- LR grids (protocol constants): LoRA {5e-5, 1e-4, 2e-4, 4e-4}; BAdam {2e-5, 1e-4, 5e-4, 2e-3}. Ranges to be sanity-anchored to the source paper's reported 7B-scale ranges at execution. Pre-registered boundary rule: if the best config is at the top grid point, extend the grid once upward (counts against budget) — prevents \"one optimizer was untuned\" artifacts.\n- BAdam defaults to verify against the reference implementation before fixing grid centers: B≈1e-3, L≈10, p≈100 (paper defaults at smaller scale; labeled memory-to-verify, not authority). Grid: B {1e-3, 1e-2}, p {100, 250}.\n- LoRA hyperparameters other than LR are fixed at the practitioner settings above (primary comparison means each method at its recommended configuration, not jointly tuned to exhaustion — symmetric treatment).\n- All other training constants identical across arms: AdamW betas/eps defaults, cosine schedule to zero after warmup (3%), bf16, global batch fixed in tokens across arms, same data order and epoch count.\n\nD4. EVALUATION (zero-shot = zero in-context exemplars; assumption labeled)\nBenchmarks: primary GSM8K-test and MATH-test; secondary out-of-domain zero-shot transfer: